select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b01-034","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What did the Accelerated Power Development & Reform Programme (2002-03) target for AT&C losses, and what did its restructured version (R-APDRP) focus on?","back":"It targeted reducing Aggregate Technical & Commercial (AT&C) losses to 15%. R-APDRP focuses on actual, demonstrable performance in loss reduction.","explain":"APDRP used technical, commercial, financial and IT interventions to improve reliability and supply quality, revenue collection and the commercial viability of State Electricity Boards. The book says capacity would have to double every 10 years to sustain growth.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p37","order":1045},
{"id":"p1-b01-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How did India's industrial/commercial electricity tariff compare internationally on a PPP basis, and how is it set?","back":"About 30.8 US cents/kWh (PPP), against 7.7 in the US, 15.3 in Japan and 20.6 in China. Tariffs are set by State Electricity Regulatory Commissions on a cost-plus basis, with industry and commerce cross-subsidising agriculture and domestic users.","explain":"HT consumers pay for both demand (kVA) and energy (kWh), while LT consumers usually pay for energy (kWh) only. Flat-rate supply to farm pumps based on horsepower means zero marginal cost, which encourages inefficient use and over-exploitation of groundwater.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p38","order":1046},
{"id":"p1-b01-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"List the key features of Availability Based Tariff (ABT).","back":"A performance-based tariff for central generating stations. A scheduling and dispatch system in which generators and beneficiaries commit to day-ahead schedules. Rewards and penalties enforce those schedules, with changes allowed if notified 1.5 hours in advance. It also emphasises prompt payment of dues.","explain":"ABT and unscheduled interchange (UI) charges were introduced in 2003 for inter-state power sale and have reduced voltage and frequency fluctuations. ABT applies to electricity, which is a frequent MCQ.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p38","order":1047},
{"id":"p1-b01-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How does the guide book (quoting UNDP's World Energy Assessment 1999) define energy security?","back":"'The continuous availability of energy in varied forms in sufficient quantities at reasonable prices.'","explain":"For a nation, the basic aim is to reduce dependence on imported energy for economic growth. India's concern is acute because about 75% of oil is imported, mostly from the volatile Middle East, and oil demand was rising about 5% a year from domestic wells over 30 years old.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p38","order":1048},
{"id":"p1-b01-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Name the four broad strategies the guide book gives for meeting India's future energy requirements.","back":"1) Reduce energy requirements. 2) Substitute imported oil/gas with domestic alternatives. 3) Diversify energy supply sources. 4) Expand the energy resource base and develop alternative sources.","explain":"Examples: efficient supercritical boilers, DSM and mass transport (reduce). Ethanol/biodiesel, biomass gasification and coal-to-oil (substitute). A fuel mix with no single dependence and oil/LNG from several countries (diversify). IOR/EOR, coal-bed methane, in-situ coal gasification and thorium reactors (expand).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p39","order":1049},
{"id":"p1-b01-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Name the benefits of energy efficiency at the levels of industry, nation and globe (Figure 1.7).","back":"Industry: lower energy bills, more competitiveness, productivity and profit, better quality. Nation: lower energy imports, avoided costs used for poverty reduction, conservation of limited resources, better energy security. Globe: less GHG and other emissions, a sustainable environment.","explain":"Energy efficiency has been practised since the 1973 oil crisis but now matters more as the most cost-effective way to mitigate climate change. Industry accounts for about 41% of global primary energy demand and a similar share of CO₂ emissions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p41","order":1050},
{"id":"p1-b01-040","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"problem","front":"Convert into tonnes of oil equivalent: (a) 10,000 kg of coal with a calorific value of 4,000 kcal/kg; (b) 10 lakh kWh of electricity.","back":"(a) 4 toe; (b) 86 toe.","explain":"1 toe = 1 × 10⁷ kcal = 11,630 kWh. Multiply quantity by calorific value to get kcal and divide by 10⁷. For electricity, use 1 kWh = 860 kcal (equivalently divide kWh by 11,630).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p43","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the toe conversion factor.","answer":"1 toe = 1 × 10⁷ kcal = 11,630 kWh","explain":"This is the standard conversion given in Section 1.2."},{"prompt":"Find the heat content of the coal.","answer":"10,000 kg × 4,000 kcal/kg = 4 × 10⁷ kcal","explain":"Energy = mass × calorific value."},{"prompt":"Convert the coal to toe.","answer":"4 × 10⁷ ÷ 10⁷ = 4 toe","explain":"Divide kcal by 10⁷ kcal/toe."},{"prompt":"Convert 10 lakh kWh to kcal.","answer":"10,00,000 kWh × 860 kcal/kWh = 8.6 × 10⁸ kcal","explain":"1 kWh = 860 kcal."},{"prompt":"Convert the electricity to toe.","answer":"8.6 × 10⁸ ÷ 10⁷ = 86 toe (check: 10⁶ ÷ 11,630 ≈ 86)","explain":"Both routes give the same answer."}],"order":1051},
{"id":"p1-b01-041","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"problem","front":"India's coal reserves at the end of 2013 were about 60,600 million t and production about 605 million t. Estimate the R/P ratio and interpret it.","back":"R/P ≈ 100 years. If production stayed at the 2013 level, reserves would last about 100 years.","explain":"R/P = reserves remaining at the end of the year ÷ that year's production. The answer matches the book's statement that Indian coal may last about 100 years. It is only a snapshot: rising production or new discoveries change the ratio every year.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p22","steps":[{"prompt":"State the R/P formula.","answer":"R/P (years) = reserves at end of year ÷ production in that year","explain":"Definition from Section 1.5."},{"prompt":"Substitute the values.","answer":"60,600 Mt ÷ 605.1 Mt/yr","explain":"Use consistent units (million tonnes)."},{"prompt":"Compute.","answer":"≈ 100 years","explain":"60,600 / 605 ≈ 100."},{"prompt":"Interpret.","answer":"Reserves would last about 100 years at constant 2013 output","explain":"R/P assumes production stays at the current level."}],"order":1052},
{"id":"p1-b01-042","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"problem","front":"An egg costs Rs 3 in India and 30 yen in Japan (30 yen ≈ Rs 15 at the market exchange rate). Find the PPP rate for eggs and explain why using the market rate would mislead.","back":"PPP = 30 yen / Rs 3 = 10 yen per rupee. The market rate (2 yen per rupee here) would overstate Japan's GDP relative to India's, because Japan's price level is higher.","explain":"A PPP exchange rate equalises the purchasing power of currencies for the same basket of goods. Using PPP values all countries' GDP at a uniform price level, so comparisons reflect real volume of output. That is why energy intensity is often given in kgoe per US$ (PPP) of GDP.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p36","steps":[{"prompt":"Set up the PPP ratio for the same good.","answer":"PPP = price in Japan / price in India = 30 yen / Rs 3","explain":"PPPs are price relatives for the same good."},{"prompt":"Compute PPP.","answer":"10 yen per rupee","explain":"For every rupee spent in India, 10 yen buys the same egg in Japan."},{"prompt":"Find the market exchange rate implied.","answer":"30 yen = Rs 15 → 2 yen per rupee","explain":"Given in the example."},{"prompt":"Explain the distortion.","answer":"At market rates Japan's high prices inflate its GDP compared with India's low-price GDP","explain":"PPP removes price-level differences, so GDP reflects real volume."}],"order":1053},
{"id":"p1-b01-043","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"problem","front":"A 60 W incandescent lamp (800 lm, 1,200 h life, 48.4 g CO₂/h) is replaced with an 8 W LED (800 lm, 25,000 h life, 6.4 g CO₂/h). For 4,000 operating hours a year, find the energy and CO₂ saved per year.","back":"Energy saved = 208 kWh/yr; CO₂ saved = 168 kg/yr.","explain":"The LED gives the same 800 lumens for about 1/8 of the power (8 W vs 60 W), which is the book's example of energy efficiency as a resource. Emissions scale with energy, so CO₂ falls by the same proportion. The LED also lasts about 20 times longer.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p41","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the power saved.","answer":"60 − 8 = 52 W","explain":"Same light output (800 lm), so the comparison is fair."},{"prompt":"Compute the annual energy saved.","answer":"52 W × 4,000 h = 208,000 Wh = 208 kWh","explain":"Energy = power × time."},{"prompt":"Compute the CO₂ saved per hour.","answer":"48.4 − 6.4 = 42 g/h","explain":"Values from Figure 1.6."},{"prompt":"Compute the annual CO₂ saved.","answer":"42 g/h × 4,000 h = 168,000 g = 168 kg","explain":"Convert grams to kilograms."}],"order":1054},
{"id":"p1-b01-044","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"problem","front":"A country produces 400 Mtoe of energy, imports 200 Mtoe, exports 50 Mtoe and withdraws a net 10 Mtoe from stocks. Its GDP at constant prices is US$ 1.4 trillion. Find gross inland consumption and energy intensity in toe per million US$.","back":"Gross inland consumption = 560 Mtoe; energy intensity = 400 toe per million US$.","explain":"Gross inland consumption = production + imports − exports + net stock withdrawals. Energy intensity = consumption ÷ GDP, expressed in toe per million US$, with GDP at constant prices so inflation does not distort the comparison.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 01 p35","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the gross inland consumption formula.","answer":"Production + imports − exports + net stock withdrawals","explain":"Definition from Section 1.11."},{"prompt":"Compute gross inland consumption.","answer":"400 + 200 − 50 + 10 = 560 Mtoe = 560 × 10⁶ toe","explain":"Exports leave the economy, so they are subtracted."},{"prompt":"Express GDP in million US$.","answer":"US$ 1.4 trillion = 1.4 × 10⁶ million US$","explain":"Match the units of the intensity ratio."},{"prompt":"Compute energy intensity.","answer":"560 × 10⁶ ÷ 1.4 × 10⁶ = 400 toe per million US$","explain":"EI = FC ÷ GDP."}],"order":1055},
{"id":"p1-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"When was the Energy Conservation Act enacted, and when did BEE come into existence?","back":"The EC Act was enacted in 2001 (in force from 1 March 2002). The Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) was set up under it on 1 March 2002, under the Ministry of Power.","explain":"BEE is the statutory body responsible for implementing the EC Act. Its mission is to develop policies and strategies, with emphasis on self-regulation and market principles, to reduce the energy intensity of the Indian economy. It does not set tariffs or give renewable capital subsidies.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q40; BEE 2025 P1 Q40","order":1056},
{"id":"p1-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Who are 'Designated Consumers' (DCs) under the EC Act, and what must they do?","back":"Large energy users in energy-intensive industries and establishments notified in the Act's Schedule. They must appoint or designate a certified energy manager, get mandatory energy audits done by an accredited energy auditor, report energy consumption, and meet the specified energy consumption norms.","explain":"Scheduled sectors include thermal power, cement, iron & steel, aluminium, fertilizer, pulp & paper, textile, chlor-alkali, petroleum refineries, railways and DISCOMs, among others. The threshold for each sector is notified in tonnes of oil equivalent per year. Non-compliance attracts penalties.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q37; BEE 2024 P1 L4","order":1057},
{"id":"p1-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What data do you need to check whether a plant qualifies as a Designated Consumer?","back":"Annual consumption of every energy source (grid electricity in kWh, coal in t, furnace oil and HSD in litres or kL, LPG or natural gas in kg or m³) together with each fuel's calorific value (and density for liquids). Convert everything to toe and compare with the notified threshold for that sector.","explain":"Total toe = Σ(quantity × GCV) ÷ 10⁷ kcal. Electricity is converted using 860 kcal/kWh. Include all purchased and self-generated energy inputs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L4","order":1058},
{"id":"p1-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Name the main measures empowered by the Energy Conservation Act 2001.","back":"Standards & Labelling of appliances, mandatory energy audits and energy managers for Designated Consumers, energy consumption norms, the Energy Conservation Building Code, and creation of BEE, State Designated Agencies and Energy Conservation Funds.","explain":"The Central Government acts through BEE, and State Governments act through State Designated Agencies (SDAs), which enforce the Act at state level. Energy Saving Certificates were added by the 2010 amendment and form the basis of PAT.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q41","order":1059},
{"id":"p1-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What were the key changes made by the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022?","back":"Power to set up a Carbon Credit Trading Scheme; mandatory minimum share of non-fossil (green hydrogen, biomass, renewable) energy for designated consumers; extension of the building code to large residential buildings as the Energy Conservation and Sustainable Building Code; Standards & Labelling extended to vehicles and vessels; enlarged BEE Governing Council; SERCs to adjudicate penalties.","explain":"The amendment moved the Act from energy efficiency alone towards decarbonisation and India's climate commitments. It gives the legal basis for the Indian Carbon Market, which issues carbon credit certificates.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1060},
{"id":"p1-040","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What penalty applies for non-compliance with EC Act provisions, as amended?","back":"Up to ₹10 lakh, plus up to ₹10,000 for each day the failure continues. A Designated Consumer that fails its energy consumption norms is also liable to an additional penalty linked to the price of each excess metric toe consumed.","explain":"These penalties are meant to make compliance cheaper than violation. Under the 2022 amendment, penalties are adjudicated by the State Electricity Regulatory Commission.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1061},
{"id":"p1-041","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the objective of BEE's Standards & Labelling (S&L) programme?","back":"To give consumers an informed choice about the energy and cost-saving potential of appliances, through star labels (1 to 5 stars; more stars means more efficient).","explain":"Launched in 2006, S&L covers appliances such as ACs, refrigerators, motors, distribution transformers and LED lamps, some under mandatory labelling. It pushes the market towards efficient products, and thresholds are tightened over time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q29","order":1062},
{"id":"p1-042","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the purpose of the Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC)?","back":"To set minimum energy-performance standards for new commercial buildings (connected load ≥ 100 kW or contract demand ≥ 120 kVA), covering envelope, HVAC, lighting, hot water and electrical systems.","explain":"ECBC was first launched in 2007 and revised in 2017. A residential code (Eco-Niwas Samhita) followed, and the 2022 amendment renamed it the Energy Conservation and Sustainable Building Code. Daylight harvesting means automatically dimming artificial lighting according to available daylight, and is a typical ECBC measure.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q41, Q42","order":1063},
{"id":"p1-096","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"What is the PAT scheme, and under which mission was it launched?","back":"Perform, Achieve and Trade: a market-based mechanism under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE), one of the 8 missions of the National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC, 2008). It sets mandatory SEC-reduction targets for Designated Consumers.","explain":"Plants that beat their target are issued Energy Saving Certificates (ESCerts). Plants that fall short must buy ESCerts or pay a penalty. ESCerts are traded on the power exchanges (IEX and PXIL).","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1064},
{"id":"p1-097","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"What is one ESCert worth?","back":"1 Energy Saving Certificate (ESCert) = 1 tonne of oil equivalent (toe) of energy saved beyond the PAT target.","explain":"Number of ESCerts = (target SEC − achieved SEC) × production in the baseline year, expressed in toe. PAT Cycle I (2012–15) covered 478 DCs in 8 sectors with a target of about 6.686 Mtoe, and achieved about 8.67 Mtoe.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1065},
{"id":"p1-098","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"Name the four initiatives under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE).","back":"1. PAT (Perform, Achieve and Trade). 2. MTEE (Market Transformation for Energy Efficiency). 3. EEFP (Energy Efficiency Financing Platform). 4. FEEED (Framework for Energy Efficient Economic Development).","explain":"PAT sets and trades efficiency targets. MTEE speeds up the shift to efficient appliances. EEFP helps finance institutions fund EE projects. FEEED provides fiscal instruments such as risk guarantee and venture funds.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1066},
{"id":"p1-099","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"What is the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS) 2023 for the Indian Carbon Market?","back":"Notified in 2023 under the EC (Amendment) Act 2022. It has a compliance mechanism, where obligated entities get GHG-emission-intensity targets, and an offset mechanism for voluntary projects. 1 carbon credit certificate = 1 tCO₂e reduced or removed.","explain":"BEE is the administrator. Grid Controller of India acts as the registry, CERC regulates trading on power exchanges, and a National Steering Committee for the Indian Carbon Market (NSC-ICM) oversees it. Obligated sectors largely carry over from PAT, moving from energy (toe) targets to emission-intensity (tCO₂e per unit of product) targets.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1067},
{"id":"p1-b02-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What are the Act number and date of the Energy Conservation Act, and of its 2010 amendment?","back":"Act 52 of 2001, dated 29-9-2001; amended by Act No. 28 of 2010, dated 4-8-2010.","explain":"The EC Act gives the legal framework and institutional arrangements for energy efficiency in India. Its first amendment came in 2010 (a frequent MCQ: options like 1948, 2003 and 2007 are distractors). The guide book (2015) reproduces the Act as amended in 2010.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p77","order":1068},
{"id":"p1-b02-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What changes did the 2010 amendment make to the Energy Conservation Act?","back":"It expanded energy conservation norms for buildings, tightened efficiency norms for appliances and equipment, and created a framework for trading energy savings between efficient and inefficient industries (energy savings certificates). It also raised penalties and provided for an Appellate Tribunal for Energy Conservation.","explain":"The trading framework (Section 14A) is the legal basis of the PAT scheme. The Appellate Tribunal hears appeals against orders of the adjudicating officer, the Central or State Government, or any other authority under the Act.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p47","order":1069},
{"id":"p1-b02-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Under the amended EC Act, what connected load or contract demand makes a commercial building a 'building' for ECBC purposes?","back":"A connected load of 100 kW or a contract demand of 120 kVA and above, used or intended for commercial purposes.","explain":"The definition covers structures after ECBC rules are notified under Sections 14(p) and 15(a), and includes existing structures. A common MCQ asks for the minimum connected load: 100 kW (not 100 kVA or 500 kW).","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p48","order":1070},
{"id":"p1-b02-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: the EC Act's definition of 'energy audit' includes the duration of the audit.","back":"False. It covers verification, monitoring and analysis of energy use, and a technical report with recommendations, cost-benefit analysis and an action plan to reduce energy consumption. Duration is not part of it.","explain":"The definition sets out what an audit must contain, not how long it takes. The manner and intervals of audits are specified separately by BEE regulations under Section 13(2)(q).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p48","order":1071},
{"id":"p1-b02-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Name five powers or functions of BEE under Section 13 of the EC Act.","back":"Any five of: recommend energy consumption norms and standards; recommend issue of energy savings certificates; recommend labelling particulars; recommend notifying designated consumers; prescribe ECBC guidelines; create awareness; arrange training; strengthen consultancy; promote R&D; develop testing and certification procedures; run pilot/demonstration projects; promote innovative financing; maintain the list of accredited energy auditors; conduct the certification exam for energy managers and auditors.","explain":"Note BEE mostly recommends while the Central Government notifies. BEE also specifies by regulation the qualifications for accreditation, the manner and intervals of energy audits, and the certification procedures for energy managers and auditors.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p48","order":1072},
{"id":"p1-b02-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Under Section 14(c), how soon after a notification can the Central Government prohibit the sale of non-conforming appliances, and can this period be extended?","back":"Not within six months of the notification. The Central Government may extend this by up to a further six months, for reasons recorded in writing.","explain":"The grace period lets manufacturers adapt. Grounds for extension include market share and technological developments affecting the equipment.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p50","order":1073},
{"id":"p1-b02-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Under Section 14(e), what factors does the Central Government consider when notifying a user as a designated consumer?","back":"The intensity or quantity of energy consumed, the investment needed to switch to energy-efficient equipment, the industry's capacity to invest, and the availability of energy-efficient machinery and equipment.","explain":"Section 14(f) also lets the Central Government alter the Schedule list of energy-intensive industries, and 14(g) lets it prescribe different norms for different DCs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p50","order":1074},
{"id":"p1-b02-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What does Section 14A of the EC Act provide?","back":"The Central Government may issue energy savings certificates to designated consumers whose consumption is below the prescribed norms. Designated consumers above the norms may buy these certificates to comply.","explain":"This is the trading mechanism added in 2010 that underpins the Perform, Achieve and Trade (PAT) scheme. Section 14B lets the Central Government, with BEE, prescribe the value per metric tonne of oil equivalent of energy consumed, used for penalties.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p51","order":1075},
{"id":"p1-b02-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Name four powers of State Governments under Section 15 of the EC Act.","back":"Any four of: amend ECBC to suit regional and local climate and notify it; direct building owners/occupiers that are DCs to comply with ECBC; direct DCs to get energy audits by accredited auditors; designate an agency to coordinate, regulate and enforce the Act in the State; create awareness; arrange training; encourage preferential treatment for efficient equipment; direct DCs to furnish energy consumption information; specify matters for inspection.","explain":"State powers mirror many Central powers but are exercised locally through the State Designated Agency, in consultation with BEE.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p52","order":1076},
{"id":"p1-b02-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the maximum penalty under Section 26(1) of the EC Act (as amended in 2010) for failing to comply with specified provisions, and for continuing failure?","back":"Up to ₹10 lakh for each failure, plus up to ₹10,000 for every day the failure continues.","explain":"It applies to failures under Section 14 clauses (c), (d), (h), (i), (k), (l), (r), (s) and Section 15 clauses (b), (c), (h). Figures are as in the 2010 text reproduced in the 2015 guide book; check later amendments separately.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p53","order":1077},
{"id":"p1-b02-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What penalty does Section 26(1A) impose for not meeting the energy consumption norms under Section 14(n)?","back":"Up to ₹10 lakh, plus, for continuing failure, an additional penalty of not less than the price of every metric tonne of oil equivalent of energy consumed in excess of the norms.","explain":"This is the penalty that applies to PAT designated consumers who miss their SEC targets. The 'price per mtoe' is the value prescribed under Section 14B.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p53","order":1078},
{"id":"p1-b02-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"If a penalty under the EC Act is not paid, how can it be recovered?","back":"As if it were an arrear of land revenue (Section 26(2)).","explain":"Treating it as land revenue lets the State use its revenue-recovery powers, such as attachment of property, without separate civil proceedings.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p53","order":1079},
{"id":"p1-b02-013","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish a 'designated agency' from a 'designated consumer' under the EC Act.","back":"A designated agency is the body a State Government designates, in consultation with BEE, to coordinate, regulate and enforce the Act within the State. A designated consumer is an energy-intensive user notified by the Central Government that must follow norms, appoint an energy manager, get audits done and report energy use.","explain":"One is a regulator and the other is regulated. DCs submit their annual energy returns and audit action reports to the designated agency, which compiles state and sectoral energy databases and gives feedback.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p53","order":1080},
{"id":"p1-b02-014","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"List four duties of a State Designated Agency.","back":"Any four of: prepare a list of designated consumers; compile DCs' annual energy returns, audit reports and action taken; prepare state and sectoral energy databases and give feedback to DCs; create awareness; arrange training; encourage preferential treatment for efficient equipment; appoint inspecting officers; help the State Government frame rules under Section 57; establish the Energy Conservation Fund.","explain":"Responsibilities (voluntary side) include spreading awareness of the Act, liaising with BEE and state departments, building staff capacity, maintaining a state website, and organising meets between energy managers, auditors and DCs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p54","order":1081},
{"id":"p1-b02-015","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What does ECBC take into account when setting norms, and who can prescribe and modify the code?","back":"It sets energy requirement norms per square metre and considers the climatic region where the building is located. The Central Government prescribes the code; State Governments can modify it to suit regional and local climatic conditions.","explain":"ECBC sets minimum efficiency standards for the design and construction of commercial buildings. It aims to minimise life-cycle (construction plus energy) costs while keeping function, comfort, health and productivity.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 02 p55","order":1082}
]$cards$::jsonb);
